text:
The King, by dividing the four types of prosperity into one, looked upon the slaughter of the lips <note>likely referring to the cessation of speech or the silencing of sentient beings' suffering</note>. Due to the karma of sentient beings, they gathered into the lotus flower possessing the marks. Even though three eons did not amount to a hundred thousand, it is like seeing a firefly at the time of the night's movement; it does not remain for long, and one does not think of it. It is thus: compassion is born for sentient beings. From the limit of the power of thought of one day, one shall examine it from one point. The clear point is that karmic ripening is self-liberated.

Through the kindness of the mind, which is without four, it is virtuous.
Karma and karmic ripening are self-liberated and virtuous.
Since the power of the <term>Om</term> <gloss>sacred syllable</gloss> has no inherent existence and is self-liberated,
Karma and karmic ripening are self-liberated and virtuous.
Since the darkness of ignorance is self-liberated,
Karma and the wind of blessings, having no inherent existence, are self-liberated;
Karma and the six perfections, having no inherent existence, are self-liberated;
Karma and the master of fire, having no inherent existence, are self-liberated;
Karma and the existence of the sound of <term>Hum</term> <gloss>sacred syllable</gloss>, having no inherent existence, are self-liberated;
Karma and the deities, having no inherent existence, are self-liberated;
Karma and meditation, having no inherent existence, are self-liberated;
Karma and emptiness, having no inherent existence, are self-liberated;
Karma and the result, having no inherent existence, are self-liberated.
Karma and karmic ripening are self-liberated and virtuous.
The karmic ripening is self-liberated and virtuous. The karmic ripening is self-liberated. It has become well-transformed.

<term>Om Mani Padme Hum</term> <gloss>the six-syllable mantra of Avalokiteshvara</gloss>. Open the six doors. Written by seal of command.

<summary>This passage describes the process of self-liberation from karma and ignorance through the realization of emptiness, emphasizing the compassionate nature of the King's teachings.</summary>
<keywords>Karma, Self-liberation, Compassion, Emptiness, Mantra, Avalokiteshvara</keywords>
